The boots pinched at the heel where the leather hadn't broken yet.

Dusty adjusted his weight on the stone road and let his gaze travel the length of the bridge. It stretched out before him — pale, enormous, old. Wide enough for twenty abreast, its guardrails carved with figures worn smooth by centuries of weather and passing hands. Beyond it, the academy island sat low against the dawn, its towers catching the first light and throwing it back in shards of white and gold.

Behind him, Aethres was waking. Somewhere in its clean, quiet streets, Rinnie was asleep in a room with a window — a real window, with glass, overlooking the lake where the sunsets painted the water in colors she'd tried to describe to him and couldn't. Clean sheets pulled to her chin. A healer who checked on her twice a day. Food she didn't have to fight for.

A week ago she'd been shivering against his ribs in a doorway in the Greys, wrapped in every scrap of cloth he owned. Her hands like ice against his chest while he held mana steady through the night — a warmth that cost him nothing, that never ran dry, that was the only thing he'd had worth giving.

Now she had sheets and food and sunsets over water. And he had new boots, a patched satchel over one shoulder, and a uniform that fit like someone else's life.

He looked forward. Adjusted the strap. Walked.

The bridge was already thick with bodies. Hundreds of students moved in a slow current toward the island, their footsteps blending into a low, unbroken murmur against the stone. Dusty fell into the flow near the edge, where the crowd thinned.

To his left, a girl in an emerald blazer was being pried from her mother's arms at the foot of the bridge. The mother's face was red and wet. The girl's wasn't — she held her jaw tight and walked forward without looking back, her hands balled at her sides. Brave, or performing brave. Either way, she managed it.

Further back, a boy wept openly, his family's crest embroidered in silver thread on his blazer. No one looked at him. Weeping nobles were afforded the same courtesy as weeping commoners — they were ignored, but for different reasons.

Others moved with practiced calm. Carriages and entourages had halted at the bridge's foot, servants unloading trunks that wouldn't follow their owners across. The rule was plain: you walked on alone. Whatever you carried, you carried yourself. Whatever you left behind stayed behind.

Dusty had nothing to leave behind.

He picked out the other lottery winners by instinct. Three, maybe four, scattered through the crowd. They moved the way he moved — close to the edges, eyes forward but awareness wide, carrying too little. Their satchels were lighter. Their uniforms fit wrong in the same ways his did — not tailored, just issued. They didn't weep. They didn't cling. They walked the way people walked when the ground behind them held nothing worth turning for.

One caught his eye — a wiry boy with cropped hair and a bruise yellowing at his jaw, half-hidden by his collar. Their gazes met for a breath. A mutual recognition, quick as a handshake, and then both looked away. Nothing to say. They already knew the same things.

The gravity shifted near the center of the bridge.

Dusty didn't look directly. He tracked it the way the Greys had taught him — peripheral vision, the movement of others around a fixed point. A cluster had formed around a young man with golden hair who walked as though the bridge had been laid to carry him somewhere important. Not arrogance, exactly. Certainty. The kind of ease that came from never once questioning whether the space you occupied was yours by right.

Four others moved with him in a formation that wasn't casual, wasn't accidental. A sandy-haired boy positioned a half-step too close, angled like a plant toward light. A dark-haired one with sharp grey eyes scanning the crowd the same way Dusty scanned it — and that made Dusty look away fast. A tall, slouching figure who seemed profoundly burdened by the act of walking. And a broad, copper-haired young man built like a furnace door, whose booming laugh carried over the murmur of the crowd like something thrown.

Other students drifted toward them. Not consciously — the way iron filings slide toward a lodestone, rearranging themselves into patterns they couldn't name. The golden-haired young man smiled at each one who drew near. Warm. Open. Generous. Dusty watched the smile land on person after person and noted that it never changed.

He dropped his gaze and kept walking.

Farther ahead, a different gravity.

She was small and slight and moved through the crowd without resistance — bodies parting for her the way water parts around a stone too smooth to disturb the current. Crimson hair fell in waves to the small of her back, catching the early light and holding it like a caught flame. Her lips were painted the same deep red. Even at this distance, even without a clear view of her face, something in Dusty's chest went still and quiet the way it did when he spotted a threat he couldn't yet name.

Men and women both orbited her, leaning in when she spoke, laughing a beat after she paused. She touched a forearm here, tilted her head there, and each gesture landed with a precision that reminded Dusty of the card hustlers in the Greys — every motion practiced until it looked unrehearsed, every reaction in the mark already mapped before it arrived.

He ducked his head.

The third was harder to read.

White hair swept into a high ponytail that hung to her thighs, pale as frost against the dark fabric of her blazer. She walked with the measured cadence of someone accustomed to being followed — chin level, gaze fixed ahead, her posture a declaration that did not require repetition. Students clustered in her vicinity but held a perimeter — near enough to be associated, far enough to never presume. It wasn't fear, exactly. It was the kind of respect that shared a border with it.

No one touched her. No one leaned close. The air around her seemed several degrees cooler than the morning warranted, and Dusty couldn't tell if that was instinct talking or something else entirely.

He filed all three in the part of his mind that catalogued exits and sight lines and people who could ruin you without raising their voice. Powerful. Orbited. The kind of people the Greys had taught him to identify early, track carefully, and never draw the attention of.

He adjusted his satchel. Lowered his gaze. Walked.

The bridge stretched longer than he'd expected. The sun climbed from the horizon by slow degrees, warming the stone beneath his new boots and turning the lake from dull iron to shimmering pale blue. The pinch at his heel settled into a rhythm he could carry.

No one spoke to him. He preferred it that way.

The murmur of the crowd shifted — a collective intake of breath, a slowing of hundreds of footsteps — and Dusty raised his head.

The academy emerged from the morning haze like something dreamed into being and then built by hands that refused to wake. Towers of pale stone rose behind curtain walls threaded with flowering ivy, their spires catching the full dawn and burning white-gold against the deepening sky. Gardens cascaded in manicured falls between buildings of marble and polished wood, their pathways lined with statues carved in such precision that the morning light lent them the illusion of breath. Courtyards opened between structures like clearings in a forest of architecture — wide, sunlit, paved in stones fitted so closely that centuries of careful tending had erased the seams. Trees heavy with late-summer leaves framed windows of leaded glass that threw fractured rainbows across walkways where no crack or weed had been permitted to survive.

And above everything, the central towers. Taller than anything Dusty had seen in his life — taller than the stacked tenements of the Greys, taller than the trade halls of the merchant quarters, taller than the cathedral spires he'd watched from alleyways as a child and wondered who they'd been built to impress. They climbed in pale stone and gleaming metal, catching the sun and holding it, marking the island's heart the way a lighthouse marks a shore: here. Everything answers to here.

He looked at it and felt nothing he could name. Not awe. Not resentment. Something closer to stepping into a room where everything was clean and knowing, with a certainty worn smooth by years of practice, that he did not belong — and that belonging had never been the point. Surviving was the point. Five years. Then Rinnie.

He kept walking.

Past the bridge's end, the current of students broke apart. Faculty in muted robes directed the flow with quiet efficiency — a pointed finger here, a murmured word there — splitting the mass into smaller tributaries. No names called. No numbers given. They were sorted the way water is sorted by stone: naturally, without discussion.

Dusty's group numbered twenty-five. They were led through a stone archway into a courtyard enclosed on three sides by buildings of warm, pale stone. A broad tree dominated the center, its canopy wide enough to throw half the space into dappled shade, its leaves catching the climbing sun in coins of gold and green. Carved benches lined the edges. The air smelled of cut grass and something floral he couldn't place.

He positioned himself near the back. Habit. Sight lines clear. Exits: the archway they'd entered through, a narrow passage between two buildings to the east, and a service door set flush into the western wall with no visible handle. Three ways out. Acceptable.

The awareness crept up the back of his neck before he could place it — that crawling certainty, honed in doorways and alleys and bad nights, that told him he was being watched. Not the scattered attention of a crowd. Specific. Weighted. A gaze with intention behind it.

He scanned the courtyard first. The other twenty-four were occupied — murmuring, shifting their weight, craning their necks at the architecture. None of them were looking at him. He lifted his gaze to the buildings. Windows. Ledges. Rooflines.

There.

A small figure sat perched at the edge of a rooftop three stories up, her legs swinging idly over the drop. Silver hair fell past her shoulders, unkempt, catching the light in a way that made it look less like hair and more like something spun from wire and left to tangle. She was tiny — slight in a way that went beyond thin, as though she'd been built at a smaller scale than everyone else and no one had thought to correct it. Her uniform was the same as theirs, but it hung on her frame like borrowed clothing.

She was looking directly at him.

Not scanning the group. Not passing her gaze across the courtyard. Looking at him — with the steady, unblinking attention of someone watching a single point for reasons she saw no need to explain. Her expression, as far as he could read it from three stories below, was nothing. Not hostile. Not curious. Present. As though she'd been sitting there for a very long time and he was simply the first thing worth settling her eyes on.

Their gazes held for a beat.

Dusty didn't look away first. In the Greys, looking away first cost you something you didn't always get back. But he didn't hold it either — he let the moment pass the way he released anything he couldn't immediately fit into a pattern. Filed it. Small. Rooftop. Watching. Didn't match anything he knew.

A soft cough broke the courtyard's quiet like a pin through silk.

His attention dropped. The other twenty-four fell silent in the same breath, which told him the sound had been designed to do exactly that.

She stood at the front of the courtyard as though she had always been there — though Dusty would have staked Rinnie's sheets and window and healer on the fact that the space had been empty a moment ago. Black dress. White apron. Skirt cut just above the knee. White stockings. Stiletto heels on courtyard stone, and not a whisper of sound to account for her arrival. Her makeup was precise — not heavy, but intentional, every line placed with the care of someone who treated appearance as a discipline rather than a habit. Dark lace gloves sheathed her hands to the wrists. Her posture was immaculate. Her expression was pleasant the way a polished door is pleasant — composed, finished, and offering nothing of what lay behind it.

"Welcome to Mythrandis." Her voice carried without effort, reaching every edge of the courtyard as clearly as if the stone itself leaned in to listen. "You will address me as Ma'am, as you will address every member of the Maid Corp you encounter during your time here. This is not a request."

She let the silence hold for precisely as long as it needed to.

"Your days will follow a rhythm. Breakfast at seven. First class at eight. Morning and early afternoon will alternate between lectures and study. Lunch at noon. Classes conclude at three. Tea is served at four — attendance is expected, not optional. Dinner at six. Curfew at ten." A pause. Slight. Perfectly weighted. "Violations of curfew will be addressed."

She did not specify how.

"You are students of Mythrandis Academy. Within these grounds, you are equals — regardless of the name you carry, the blood in your veins, or the depth of the mana you hold." Her gaze swept the group without lingering on any one of them, and yet Dusty had the distinct, uncomfortable certainty that she had looked at every single one of them individually. "You will conduct yourselves accordingly. How thoroughly you believe that is, of course, your own affair."

Her gloved hands folded at her waist. A deliberate pause — not hesitation. Punctuation.

"I will now show you the areas relevant to your first week. You will follow. You will not wander. You will not touch what has not been offered to you."

She turned on her heel — the stiletto striking the stone with a single, clean note — and walked. She did not look back to confirm they followed.

They followed.

She led them through corridors of polished stone and open walkways where the morning sun fell in warm shafts through arched windows. Gardens appeared and vanished between buildings — roses climbing trellises of dark iron, beds of herbs releasing their scent as the group passed close. Dusty caught lavender, something sharp and green beneath it, and a sweet resinous note he associated with the poultice work he'd taught himself from a healer's discarded manual in the Greys.

She pointed. She named. Dining halls — seven of them, spread throughout the central grounds, each large enough to seat hundreds beneath vaulted wooden ceilings darkened with age. The dormitories to the north, where room assignments would arrive by evening. The Solarium to the east — open-aired, sun-drenched, thick with greenery — where the healing faculty practiced their craft. Libraries that climbed three and four stories high, their tall windows glowing amber, their interiors visible through glass: shelves upon shelves rising toward ceilings painted with scenes too distant to discern.

She walked and they followed, and not once did her pace suggest she cared whether they kept up. She simply moved, and the twenty-five adjusted. Dusty noted that even the nobles among them — six, by his count, sorted by the quality of their boots and the way they held their shoulders — offered no complaint. Whatever they'd been before they crossed the bridge, whatever titles and estates and retinues waited on the far side of the water, here they were twenty-five students trailing a woman in stiletto heels who had not once invited questions.

The central courtyard opened wide before them — the largest space Dusty had seen on the island. Pale stone paving stretched in every direction, catching and holding the full weight of the climbing sun. And from its heart, the central tower rose.

It climbed above everything else. Its stone shifted from warm cream at the base to near-white at its peak, where it met the sun and held it until the tower itself seemed to burn. Windows of leaded glass marked its ascent at even intervals, catching light, giving back fractured color across the courtyard below. At its apex, a single balcony overlooked the island in every direction — north, south, east, and west — seeing all, answerable to nothing.

The Maid stopped. The twenty-five stopped behind her.

"The central tower." Her voice carried something it hadn't before — not reverence, not warmth, but a weight beneath the professionalism that said the words were not casual. "The Headmaster's office and personal quarters occupy the uppermost floors. You will not enter the tower without explicit invitation." She paused. "Such invitations are rare."

The silence settled across the courtyard like something poured. The tower's shadow stretched long across the sun-warmed stone, its edge reaching forward until it touched the toes of Dusty's new boots.

He looked up at the balcony. Empty. The glass caught the light and gave back nothing.

He adjusted his satchel, felt the pinch at his heel, and followed.
